<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

    <!--
      Backup is off, deliberately and explicitly.

      `allowBackup` defaults to true. Left alone, Android Auto Backup copies the
      Hive database — document metadata and the full recognised text of every
      scan — plus the page images and generated PDFs to the user's Google Drive.
      DocuAI's entire premise is that documents never leave the device, so the
      default is not merely untidy here: it is the one setting that would make
      the privacy claim false.

      `tools:replace` is present because a merged library manifest could
      otherwise reintroduce the default; this makes ours authoritative.
    -->
    <application
        android:label="DocuAI"
        android:name="${applicationName}"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="true"
        tools:replace="android:allowBackup"
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules">
        <!--
          `launchMode` and `taskAffinity` are both load-bearing for "Open with".

          **singleTask, not singleTop.** singleTop was wrong, and the symptom
          was that opening a ZIP from WhatsApp put DocuAI's archive browser
          *inside WhatsApp's task*: the card in Recents carried WhatsApp's icon,
          and backing out of the browser landed in WhatsApp rather than in
          DocuAI.

          The reason is that `taskAffinity` decides almost nothing on its own.
          Android consults it only when the incoming intent carries
          FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK — and a sharing app that calls
          `startActivity(chooser)` from one of its own activities does not set
          it. Without that flag a `standard` or `singleTop` activity is simply
          placed on top of the caller's stack, affinity unread.

          `singleTask` is the launch mode that cannot be placed in somebody
          else's task. The system always hosts it in a task matching its own
          affinity, creating one if there is none, whatever flags the caller
          used — and it still delivers a second file to the running instance
          through `onNewIntent`, which is what this feature needs.

          The affinity below stays, and is now doing the job it is named for:
          it is the affinity singleTask hosts this activity under. It was `""`
          in the Flutter scaffold, which would leave singleTask hosting the
          activity in an unnamed task that nothing can be matched back to.

          What that gives up is a mitigation for task-affinity hijacking
          (StrandHogg), where a hostile app declares our affinity to get its own
          activity placed in our task. Android 11 closed that at the platform
          level, and an app that is a share target — which this one now is —
          needs a real affinity regardless. Written out as the package name
          rather than deleted, so the next reader finds a decision instead of an
          omission.
        -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:launchMode="singleTask"
            android:taskAffinity="com.sidrahayat.docuai"
            android:theme="@style/LaunchTheme"
            android:configChanges="orientation|keyboardHidden|keyboard|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|locale|layoutDirection|fontScale|screenLayout|density|uiMode"
            android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize">
            <!-- Specifies an Android theme to apply to this Activity as soon as
                 the Android process has started. This theme is visible to the user
                 while the Flutter UI initializes. After that, this theme continues
                 to determine the Window background behind the Flutter UI. -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="io.flutter.embedding.android.NormalTheme"
              android:resource="@style/NormalTheme"
              />

            <!--
              Flutter must not turn an incoming file into a route.

              This defaults to *true* when the key is absent, and what it does
              is take `intent.getData()` and hand the whole URI to the framework
              as the app's initial route. For an app with App Links that is the
              feature; for this one it means GoRouter is asked to navigate to
              `content://com.whatsapp.provider.media/item/e417…` and shows "no
              routes for location" instead of the archive browser.

              It cannot be worked around in code. The engine is created — and
              the Dart entrypoint *run*, with that route already baked in —
              inside `FlutterActivityAndFragmentDelegate.onAttach`, which
              happens before `configureFlutterEngine` is ever called. There is
              no earlier hook to clear the intent from.

              Nothing is lost by turning it off. DocuAI has no App Links, no
              custom scheme and no web presence; every route it has is pushed
              from inside the app. Files arriving from other apps come through
              the `com.sidrahayat.docuai/incoming` channel instead, which reads
              the same intent — see IncomingFiles.kt.
            -->
            <meta-data
              android:name="flutter_deeplinking_enabled"
              android:value="false"
              />
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER"/>
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              "Open with DocuAI", from a file manager, a chat app or a browser
              download.

              Three filters rather than one, because Android matches an action
              against the *whole* filter: ACTION_VIEW carries a URI and needs a
              scheme to match on, ACTION_SEND carries its URI as an extra and
              would never match a filter that demanded one, and
              ACTION_SEND_MULTIPLE is deliberately narrower than either.

              `android:launchMode="singleTask"` above is what makes these work
              while the app is already open, and what keeps the app out of the
              sending app's task. See the note on the activity.
            -->

            <!--
              Tapping a file somewhere else and choosing DocuAI.

              The scheme and mimeType lists multiply out: every scheme is
              matched against every type, which is why both live in one filter
              rather than fourteen. `file` is here for the handful of older
              file managers that still send one; everything modern sends
              `content`.

              BROWSABLE is present so a download notification or a link opened
              in Chrome can hand the file over. It is scoped by the schemes
              above — there is no http or https data element here, so this
              cannot be used to make DocuAI open a URL off the network.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/open_with_label">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE"/>

                <data android:scheme="content"/>
                <data android:scheme="file"/>

                <data android:mimeType="application/zip"/>
                <!-- What Windows-made ZIPs, and most of WhatsApp, report. -->
                <data android:mimeType="application/x-zip-compressed"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/jpeg"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/png"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/webp"/>
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
            </intent-filter>

            <!-- Sharing one file *to* DocuAI from another app's share sheet. -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/open_with_label">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>

                <data android:mimeType="application/zip"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/x-zip-compressed"/>
                <data android:mimeType="application/pdf"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/jpeg"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/png"/>
                <data android:mimeType="image/webp"/>
                <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
            </intent-filter>

            <!--
              Several files at once, images only.

              Narrower than the filters above on purpose: a multi-share of
              pictures is a document waiting to happen — the app already builds
              one out of several photographs — whereas five PDFs and a ZIP
              shared together have no single sensible outcome.
            -->
            <intent-filter android:label="@string/open_with_label">
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.SEND_MULTIPLE"/>
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT"/>

                <data android:mimeType="image/*"/>
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
        <!--
          Lends one file at a time to another app.

          Needed by "Open with another app" in the archive browser: a file
          inside DocuAI's private storage cannot be handed over as a path — no
          other app can read it — so it is handed over as a content URI with a
          one-shot read grant, which the system revokes when the other app is
          done with it.

          `exported="false"` with `grantUriPermissions="true"` is the pair that
          makes this safe: nothing can query this provider on its own initiative,
          and the only URIs that exist are the ones this app mints and attaches
          to an intent the user themselves chose an app for. What may be minted
          is fixed by docuai_file_paths.xml.
        -->
        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="com.sidrahayat.docuai.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/docuai_file_paths"/>
        </provider>

        <!-- Don't delete the meta-data below.
             This is used by the Flutter tool to generate GeneratedPluginRegistrant.java -->
        <meta-data
            android:name="flutterEmbedding"
            android:value="2" />
    </application>
    <!-- Required to query activities that can process text, see:
         https://developer.android.com/training/package-visibility and
         https://developer.android.com/reference/android/content/Intent#ACTION_PROCESS_TEXT.

         In particular, this is used by the Flutter engine in io.flutter.plugin.text.ProcessTextPlugin. -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.PROCESS_TEXT"/>
            <data android:mimeType="text/plain"/>
        </intent>
    </queries>
</manifest>
